<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for the Developer ID build, applied together with the hardened runtime
  (`codesign --options runtime`). Notarization REQUIRES the hardened runtime, and the
  hardened runtime breaks CoreCLR unless these three are granted — which is why they are
  here and not a wish list:

    allow-jit                        CoreCLR compiles IL at run time. Without this the
                                     app dies at startup mapping executable pages.
    allow-unsigned-executable-memory CoreCLR also writes into pages it then executes
                                     (stubs, write-barriers). JIT alone is not enough.
    disable-library-validation       The bundle loads ~19 dylibs (Skia, HarfBuzz,
                                     libcoreclr, Avalonia's native layer) that carry
                                     Microsoft's and our signature, not one team's.

  This is the reason the agent could never be a Mac App Store app even if the proxy
  helper and the System-keychain CA were removed: App Review does not grant
  allow-unsigned-executable-memory to a sandboxed app, so a self-contained CoreCLR build
  has nowhere to go. Developer ID is the channel; see ../README.md.

  Deliberately absent: com.apple.security.app-sandbox. The agent installs a root helper,
  rewrites system-wide proxies and writes a LaunchAgent — all of which the sandbox
  forbids, and none of which an entitlement can buy back.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
